            <ArticleTitle>Effects of Case Studies and Simulated Patients on Students' Nursing Care Plan.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>87-94</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/2047-3095.12080</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study is to determine the effects of using case studies and simulated patients in teaching students to plan their nursing care.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The study sample consisted of 70 second-year nursing students. Study data were collected using a questionnaire and by evaluating students' nursing care plans.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The highest percentages of diagnoses identified were altered oral mucous membrane, ineffective breathing pattern, disturbed sleep pattern: less than body requirement, impaired tissue integrity, pain, and risk for infection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The use of simulated patients in nursing education appears to help students evaluate realistic medical cases and clinical problems, and plan their nursing care.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2015 NANDA International, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
